WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:02.310
INTRO:
For anyone who’s been involved in the piracy

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:07.250
community, KickassTorrents or KAT, is one
of the most classic torrenting sites in the

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:09.980
world right up there with The Pirate Bay.

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:15.740
In fact, in 2015, KAT became the most popular
file-sharing website in the world as The Pirate

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:18.250
Bay suffered from legal consequences.

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:23.199
Unfortunately for KAT, their time at the top
didn’t last too long as they started to

00:00:23.199 --> 00:00:26.050
experience their own legal issues soon after.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:30.280
Nevertheless, KAT has remained an extremely
popular option amongst pirates, allowing them

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:33.190
to easily secure their spot in the hall of
infamy.

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:37.690
But as we saw with the Pirate Bay’s founders
and MegaUpload’s founder, prosecutors and

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:41.460
copyright holders aren’t exactly kind to
such founders.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:46.210
After all, New Zealand sent their anti-terrorist
squad against MegaUpload’s founder, and

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:48.700
Sweden chased the Pirate Bay founders across
the world.

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:53.780
So, there’s no doubt that if you found one
of these sites, you wanna remain anonymous,

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:56.910
and that’s exactly what KAT’s founder
tried to do.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:01:03.550
But, prosecutors did eventually find out who
he was, and here’s what happened next.

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:08.030
INCOMPLIANCE:
Taking a look back, the story of KickassTorrents

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:13.110
circles back to a man named Artem Vaulin,
or at least that’s what prosecutors allege.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:18.000
Ethnically, Artem was Ukrainian, but it looks
like he lived out most of his life in Poland.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.530
It’s not clear when exactly Artem was born,
but given that he was 30 years old when he

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:28.310
was arrested in 2016, we can deduct that he
was born in 1986 or 1987.

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:32.660
Anyway, it looks like Artem was heavily involved
in the piracy community during his teenage

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:33.660
years.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:38.159
And it didn’t take him very long to switch
from being a user to being a host as Artem

00:01:38.159 --> 00:01:41.170
spotted a business opportunity within the
piracy scene.

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:45.520
Similar to YouTube and Instagram, if you hosted
a piracy site, you could display ads that

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:50.550
could drive in revenue, and as you’ll see
later, for KAT, this added up to quite a bit

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:51.550
of revenue.

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:54.990
Considering the potential, Artem didn’t
waste any time and he quickly put his coding

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:56.040
skills to work.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:00.700
Soon after, in November of 2008, Artem launched
KickassTorrents.

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:05.210
Initially, it looks like Artem tried his best
to avoid issues with the authorities.

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:09.240
KAT proudly claimed that it complied with
the DMCA and that they would remove copyright

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.460
infringing torrents as soon as they were notified.

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:15.160
But, in reality, this wasn’t exactly the
case.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:19.400
Given the context of the site, it didn’t
take long for users to start uploading copyrighted

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:22.480
content, and for copyright holders to start
contacting KAT.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:25.880
But, they were hit with the same response
every single time.

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:30.690
Greetings, your request has been reviewed,
but cannot be processed due to one or more

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:31.930
of the following reasons.

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:35.810
And most of these reasons were complete and
utter BS.

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:39.569
Reason 4, for example, was that there were
more than 30 torrents mentioned in the claim

00:02:39.569 --> 00:02:40.569
email.

00:02:40.569 --> 00:02:44.990
If KAT was legitimately trying to comply,
addressing such a claim would be a top priority.

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:49.900
But, KAT was intentionally avoiding claims
with large lists of torrents, probably because

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:53.750
they wanted to draw out the takedown process
and minimize how many torrents they lost with

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:55.120
each takedown.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:59.209
KAT was able to get away with such outright
incompliance for many years as prosecutors

00:02:59.209 --> 00:03:00.940
had much bigger fish to fry.

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:05.410
But as soon as they took down MegaUpload and
The Pirate Bay, prosecutors turned their focus

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:07.540
to KickassTorrents.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:12.030
ANONYMOUS BATTLE:
Initially, prosecutors started off pretty

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:13.790
light against Kickass.

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:18.260
They didn’t know who was behind the site,
and honestly, I don’t think they cared.

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:22.190
They just wanted to take down the website
and stop the spread of copyrighted material.

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:27.320
So, in February of 2013, they started working
with internet service providers to block access

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:28.470
to KAT.

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:32.610
The High Court in London ordered ISPs to block
people from accessing KickassTorrents and

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:34.100
two other torrent sites.

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:39.130
The ISPs complied and blocked access, but
every time they blocked access to one domain

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:42.370
of Kickass, Kickass just moved to another
domain.

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:49.480
They moved from ka.tt to kickass.to to kickass.so
to kickasstorrents.im to kat.cr and so on

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:50.830
and so forth.

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:54.950
Clearly, prosecutors couldn’t take down
the website through ISPs, but they were able

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:57.670
to force KAT to constantly switch domains.

00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:00.790
This meant that most people didn’t know
what the latest domain was off the top of

00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:01.790
their heads.

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:04.940
They had to Google KickassTorrents to find
the latest link.

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:09.800
Considering this, the Motion Picture Association
of America or the MPAA decided to work with

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:11.590
Google to take down KAT.

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:15.010
They asked Google to remove the correct kickass
link from Google search results.

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:19.549
They also worked with Steam to prevent the
correct link from being shared on Steam chat.

00:04:19.549 --> 00:04:23.479
This strategy was actually decently effective
as this resulted in many pirates stumbling

00:04:23.479 --> 00:04:27.210
upon malicious copycat sites that were filled
with malware and viruses.

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:31.360
And this was the perfect excuse for Chrome
and Firefox to completely block access to

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:33.509
KAT even if you had the correct link.

00:04:33.509 --> 00:04:37.669
If you tried to access KAT through these browsers
in late 2015, you got a message suggesting

00:04:37.669 --> 00:04:41.699
that they blocked access to the site because
they didn’t want you to download a virus.

00:04:41.699 --> 00:04:45.950
It’s definitely not because they were being
pressured by copyright holders, they just

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:48.539
wanted to look out for your best interests.

00:04:48.539 --> 00:04:51.699
Jokes aside though, we all know why they blocked
access.

00:04:51.699 --> 00:04:56.949
In 2016, Chrome and Firefox changed their
excuse to phishing concerns, but soon enough,

00:04:56.949 --> 00:04:58.960
neither of them wanted to play these charades
anymore.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:02.309
So, they opened up access and stepped out
of the way.

00:05:02.309 --> 00:05:06.289
By this point, it was clear that KAT wasn’t
just a bug that prosecutors could squash and

00:05:06.289 --> 00:05:07.599
move on.

00:05:07.599 --> 00:05:11.819
KAT had evolved into being the world’s largest
file-sharing site, so prosecutors decided

00:05:11.819 --> 00:05:14.460
that it was time to go for the host.

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:19.550
IRS SPY:
By this point, the FBI had spent years trying

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:24.849
to unsuccessfully takedown KAT, so, they decided
to escalate things to the real top dogs: the

00:05:24.849 --> 00:05:25.849
IRS.

00:05:25.849 --> 00:05:29.690
Whether it be Al Capone or the Wolf of Wall
Street, it’s clear that the one entity that

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:32.759
you never wanna piss off is the IRS.

00:05:32.759 --> 00:05:37.759
Many have pissed off the FBI and gotten away
with it for years, but almost no one has escaped

00:05:37.759 --> 00:05:41.009
the IRS, and this was no different for Artem.

00:05:41.009 --> 00:05:44.710
The IRS didn’t care that Artem was causing
billions of dollars worth of lost revenue

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:46.430
for copyright holders.

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:50.599
But they did care that Artem was causing losses
for the IRS.

00:05:50.599 --> 00:05:55.129
You see, Artem made money off displaying ads
on KAT, and many of these advertisers were

00:05:55.129 --> 00:05:56.129
American.

00:05:56.129 --> 00:06:01.550
Yet, Artem wasn’t paying sales tax or income
tax on this revenue which was a fatal mistake.

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:06.919
In March of 2016, the IRS got an undercover
agent to inquire about an ad on KAT.

00:06:06.919 --> 00:06:11.539
The IRS agent was able to get in contact with
a KAT employee named Mr. White.

00:06:11.539 --> 00:06:16.800
Mr. White informed the agent that KAT charges
$300 per ad per day and gave the agent payment

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:18.460
details to a Latvian bank.

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:23.069
The agent went ahead and bought a five-day
ad in order to gain the trust of KAT and potentially

00:06:23.069 --> 00:06:24.409
get more details.

00:06:24.409 --> 00:06:29.669
Clearly, the IRS had motives behind paying
KAT, but technically, this does also mean

00:06:29.669 --> 00:06:32.460
that the IRS supported KAT with $1500.

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:37.939
Anyway, shortly after, the IRS agent contacted
Mr. White again to buy a more expensive ad.

00:06:37.939 --> 00:06:42.229
This time he was looking at a banner ad that
cost $1000 to $3200 per day.

00:06:42.229 --> 00:06:46.500
It’s not clear if the IRS actually made
the purchase, but they were able to uncover

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:48.899
a new bank account based in Estonia.

00:06:48.899 --> 00:06:52.820
Using these details in conjunction with the
Mutual Legal Assitance Treaty, investigators

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:56.229
were able to obtain all the banking records
of these two accounts.

00:06:56.229 --> 00:06:59.499
This helped the IRS figure out that Artem
was making bank off of KAT.

00:06:59.499 --> 00:07:04.279
At the peak, KAT was pulling in nearly $17
million in annual revenue.

00:07:04.279 --> 00:07:09.669
The IRS wanted their cut, and fortunately
for them, the bank accounts led to more clues.

00:07:09.669 --> 00:07:12.500
Investigators were able to uncover that the
operation was being hosted out of Chicago

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:14.899
and that the servers were located in Canada.

00:07:14.899 --> 00:07:18.759
But, the most important piece of evidence
they found was Artem’s IP address.

00:07:18.759 --> 00:07:22.770
They didn’t know who the IP address belonged
to, but the walls were quickly closing in

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:25.279
on Artem.

00:07:25.279 --> 00:07:29.619
ARRESTED:
Artem had no idea that prosecutors were so

00:07:29.619 --> 00:07:33.919
close to uncovering his identity, so he continued
on with his daily life.

00:07:33.919 --> 00:07:36.849
And this included purchasing media from the
iTunes store.

00:07:36.849 --> 00:07:41.499
Ironically, the owner of the largest piracy
website in the world was paying for his own

00:07:41.499 --> 00:07:42.499
media.

00:07:42.499 --> 00:07:45.370
I guess that he felt that if you had the money,
you should pay for it.

00:07:45.370 --> 00:07:49.699
I’m not sure, but this ironically ended
up being the root of his demise.

00:07:49.699 --> 00:07:54.150
You see, investigators were working with a
bunch of companies to track down the IP address,

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:55.339
one of which was Apple.

00:07:55.339 --> 00:08:01.149
And, when Artem made a purchase on the iTunes
store using his Apple ID and his IP address,

00:08:01.149 --> 00:08:03.270
prosecutors were quickly able to identify
him.

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:07.259
Facebook was also able to provide evidence
that it was in fact Artem and the same IP

00:08:07.259 --> 00:08:10.159
address that was operating KAT’s official
Facebook account.

00:08:10.159 --> 00:08:15.289
And on July 20, 2016, the Department of Justice
proudly announced that they had uncovered

00:08:15.289 --> 00:08:18.830
the identity of Artem and that Poland police
had arrested him.

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:23.559
They were also able to shut down KAT’s proxy
servers and officially take down the website.

00:08:23.559 --> 00:08:26.669
Prosecutors also went ahead and stacked on
every copyright infringement and money laundering

00:08:26.669 --> 00:08:27.839
charge they could think of.

00:08:27.839 --> 00:08:32.669
And these were pretty severe given that KAT
had distributed $1 billion worth of media.

00:08:32.669 --> 00:08:36.760
According to Artem’s lawyer Ira Rothken,
they were looking to charge up to 20 years

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:40.950
for the money laundering and up to 5 years
for each count of copyright infringement.

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:45.800
Rothken did clarify that he highly doubted
that all these convictions would go through,

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:49.910
but worst-case scenario, Artem could’ve
been looking at decades in jail.

00:08:49.910 --> 00:08:53.570
To make things worse, Artem was suffering
from extreme back pain and had to be taken

00:08:53.570 --> 00:08:54.570
to the hospital.

00:08:54.570 --> 00:08:58.900
Artem’s describes that during this trip
to the hospital, he was escorted by four policemen

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:00.350
with machines guns.

00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:03.960
And one of the guards told him that they heard
that he committed three murders.

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:08.310
Hearing this, Artem knew that this was going
to be more of a criminal lawsuit than a civil

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:12.700
lawsuit, and he had no intention of sticking
around for the verdict.

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:17.160
KAT TODAY:
As you might’ve already guessed, as soon

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:20.690
as bail was posted, Artem paid the amount
and fled the country.

00:09:20.690 --> 00:09:24.430
The bail amount was surprisingly only $108,000.

00:09:24.430 --> 00:09:28.570
For someone who was making tens of millions
per year, forfeiting even ten million would’ve

00:09:28.570 --> 00:09:30.200
been an easy price to pay for freedom.

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:33.970
But, luckily for Artem, he only had to pay
a little over a hundred thousand.

00:09:33.970 --> 00:09:39.330
It’s not clear how exactly Artem left Poland
given that his passport was confiscated, but

00:09:39.330 --> 00:09:43.750
given his wealth, I’m sure he was easily
able to find a 1st class smuggler.

00:09:43.750 --> 00:09:48.740
Anyway, on August 26, 2020, the Polish Ministry
of Justice confirmed that Artem had fled the

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:53.380
country and that extradition proceedings would
no longer be continuing in Poland.

00:09:53.380 --> 00:09:57.500
His legal team withdrew from the case after
they lost contact with Artem, and the Artem

00:09:57.500 --> 00:09:59.950
prosecution has since been put on hold.

00:09:59.950 --> 00:10:04.020
In other words, as long as Artem is never
caught, the case will never reach a verdict,

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:08.250
and Artem can more or less live a free life
in some non-extraditing country.

00:10:08.250 --> 00:10:12.180
As for KickassTorrents today, there are many
sites trying to emulate the original, but

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:15.670
the consensus seems to be that none of them
are as good as the original.

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:19.770
Also, one of the main reasons that Kickass
became so popular was because the Pirate Bay

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:24.250
was taken down, so naturally, everyone flocked
to the second-best option.

00:10:24.250 --> 00:10:28.490
But, with the Pirate Bay basically becoming
immortal, there seems to be little need for

00:10:28.490 --> 00:10:29.530
anything else.

00:10:29.530 --> 00:10:33.040
Still, many miss the glory days of KAT and
the community behind it.

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:37.010
While KAT was not able to become immortal
like the Pirate Bay, there’s the doubt that

00:10:37.010 --> 00:10:40.880
KAT will permanently be remembered as a legend
in the piracy community.

